# mindex project marker (YAML). Read by mindex-index, mindex-watch, the VS Code
# extension and the post-commit hook; the MCP servers don't parse it — the agent
# reads it and passes the GUID and filters as call arguments. One file, at the
# repo root, no nesting.
#
# Keys: guid (required, dashed or dashless UUID), plus the optional scope lists
# exclude_paths / include_paths / languages. An unknown key is an error, not a
# silent no-op — a mistyped `exclude_path:` would otherwise index the tree it was
# meant to keep out. Globs are root-relative with forward slashes; `*` stops at a
# directory separator, `**` crosses them; excludes are applied before includes, so
# an include cannot re-admit what an exclude dropped.
#
# Scope policy for this repo: index everything that is written, exclude only what
# is generated, fetched or secret. That deliberately includes the tooling — the
# indexer, watcher, mindexfile, both MCP servers, the VS Code extension, the
# embedder, the shell scripts, the SQL migrations and the documentation — because
# all of it is code someone has to answer questions about.

guid: c2d7e2c1-3165-42f5-9366-0ff1492b4bab

exclude_paths:
  # Git internals.
  - .git/**
  # Rust build output: the root workspace plus the three tools/ crates, which are
  # separate workspaces with their own target/ each.
  - target/**
  - "**/target/**"
  # VS Code extension: npm deps, packaged builds, and everything the build
  # produces. The list mirrors tools/vscode/.gitignore, which is the authority on
  # what in that directory is generated — out/ is the tsc output duplicating every
  # src/ file, media/js/ is the esbuild bundle of src/webview, and media/codicons/
  # is a vendored VS Code font. The hand-written media files (ask.css, lang.css,
  # the *.html shells) stay in: they are authored and CLAUDE.md discusses them.
  - "**/node_modules/**"
  - "**/dist/**"
  - "**/*.vsix"
  - tools/vscode/out/**
  - tools/vscode/media/js/**
  - tools/vscode/media/codicons/**
  # The Grafana dashboard, generated by the gen_dashboard.py sitting next to it —
  # 141 kB of panel JSON, 132 chunks, and the single largest file in the index
  # after this exclusion was found. The generator is the authored artefact and
  # stays in; the dashboard is its output, and answers no question the generator
  # does not answer better.
  - deploy/grafana/mindex-service.json
  # Python environments and tool caches. The embedder's venvs are per-backend
  # (.venv-egpu / .venv-igpu), so the plain `.venv` glob does not reach them.
  - "**/.venv/**"
  - "**/.venv-*/**"
  - "**/__pycache__/**"
  - "**/.ruff_cache/**"
  - "**/.mypy_cache/**"
  - "**/.pytest_cache/**"
  - "**/.ipynb_checkpoints/**"
  # Lockfiles: machine-generated dependency graphs, no prose, and large enough to
  # crowd out real code. `*.lock` covers Cargo.lock, poetry.lock and uv.lock;
  # npm's is not matched by it and sliced to 117 chunks — a sixth of the whole
  # index — before it was excluded by name.
  - "**/*.lock"
  - "**/package-lock.json"
  # The perf harness's benchmark corpus: cloned third-party repositories, i.e.
  # foreign source code and the single biggest polluter of this index. The
  # harness itself (fetch.sh, run.sh, repos.txt) stays in.
  - perf/corpus/.clones/**
  - perf/corpus/data/**
  # Benchmark output: one CSV per run, plus generated plots.
  - perf/results/**
  - perf/plots/**
  # Secrets and generated certificates.
  - "**/*.pem"
  - certs/**
  # Agent configuration. Not all of .claude/**: CLAUDE.md is this project's
  # densest documentation and answers "why is it built this way" about as well as
  # the source does, so it is indexed. Settings and agent definitions are
  # configuration for a tool that reads this repo, not a description of it.
  - .claude/settings*.json
  - .claude/agents/**

# Empty means the whole tree (minus the excludes above) — which is the point:
# nothing here is narrowed to a subset.
include_paths: []

# Empty means every language mindex supports, which is what this repo needs: Rust,
# Python, TypeScript, JavaScript, bash, SQL, YAML/TOML/JSON and Markdown all carry
# something worth retrieving. The canonical list is served at GET /config.
languages: []
